## Primary sources and reuse (12 October 2026)

- [EDRDG license](https://www.edrdg.org/edrdg/licence.html), verified current CC BY-SA 4.0. Preserve attribution and license references; distributed data adaptations retain share-alike. Its update clause requires a regular update procedure, and specifies monthly for web dictionary services. Software can be proprietary; existing data cannot be rebranded as owned exclusively by ZenRay. French gloss authors retain their own copyright. (12 October 2026)
- [Producer JSON conversion](https://github.com/scriptin/jmdict-simplified) distributes the converted dictionary under the source terms and publishes weekly archives. This is actual lexicographic data, with stable IDs and self-contained sense fields, rather than generated prose. (12 October 2026)
- [KanjiVG](https://kanjivg.tagaini.net/) is Ulrich Apel and contributors, CC BY-SA 3.0. Stroke SVGs preserve path order and component labels. Use original vectors and derive diagrams; preserve attribution and indicate modifications. (12 October 2026)
- [Tatoeba official downloads](https://tatoeba.org/en/downloads) licenses sentence exports CC BY 2.0 FR; preserve both authors and original sentence URLs. Audio is licensed separately per contributor; none is downloaded by this research task. Community correctness remains distinct from editorial review. (12 October 2026)
- [Official JLPT FAQ](https://www.jlpt.jp/e/faq/) says modern exhaustive vocabulary, grammar and kanji lists are not published. Level coverage must be an editorial curriculum with external evidence, rather than a claim of exact official lists. Test materials stay links unless reuse permission is checked. (12 October 2026)

## Parser schema and deterministic assembly (12 October 2026)

- JMdict root `words`; each `id` has arrays `kanji`, `kana`, `sense`. Choose a spelling and a reading compatible with `kana[].appliesToKanji`; preserve `sense[].appliesToKanji` and `appliesToKana` so a gloss is not assigned to the wrong reading. French language code is `fre`, English `eng`. Root `tags` expands abbreviated POS, register, domain and dialect labels. (12 October 2026)
- French and English senses can be independently split. Do not zip same-position senses across languages as equivalent meanings. A French-first display falls back to clearly labeled English; do not silently machine-translate. (12 October 2026)
- KANJIDIC2 root `characters`; each has `literal`, `radicals`, `misc` and `readingMeaning`. Japanese readings use `ja_on` and `ja_kun`; French meanings use `fr`, English `en`. `misc.strokeCounts[0]` is accepted count, later alternatives can be miscounts. (12 October 2026)
- `misc.jlptLevel` is the old pre-2010 four-level system. Preserve it as legacy provenance; never map 1 directly into modern N1 and 2 into modern N2. [KANJIDIC documentation](https://www.edrdg.org/wiki/KANJIDIC_Project.html) explicitly explains the historical field. (12 October 2026)
- KanjiVG filenames are Unicode lowercase hex padded to five characters, e.g. `065e5.svg`. SVG paths ending in `-s1`, `-s2`, etc. give ordered strokes. `kvg:element` gives components. Some KANJIDIC characters lack a main SVG; report that absence. (12 October 2026)
- Tatoeba detailed TSV is `id, language, text, username, createdAt, modifiedAt`; direct links join Japanese ID to French ID. `tatoeba-jpn-fra.jsonl` provides one pair per direct link and preserves author attribution and URL on both sides. Missing linked sentences are counted, not invented. (12 October 2026)
- Import stable IDs and original source payload into SQLite; FTS5 supports French normalized glosses and kana/kanji. Exact kanji and reading lookup must be indexed separately because Japanese text without spaces is poorly represented by default FTS tokenization. Browser publication should use lazy shards plus a compact routing index; full datasets remain in the mapped Assets sources. (12 October 2026)
- A dictionary lookup can produce a vocabulary lesson, but cannot prove a grammar explanation, natural pragmatic use, learned mastery or cultural generalization. Link each grammar or Japan claim to separately checked sources, and keep personal mastery in Japanese1. (12 October 2026)

